  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates a protein-based redox mechanism in which a conformational change controls both proton and electron movement to an embedded metal cofactor. A large protein-like shape surrounds a central site labeled with a metal, shown coordinated by several ligands. Curved arrows indicate that the protein changes shape, apparently repositioning key functional groups around the cofactor.\n\nOn the left, several H+ symbols and a pink arrow suggest proton transfer into or out of the active site. Nearby carboxylate and hydroxyl-containing groups appear to form part of the proton relay network. Inside the protein, aromatic groups and purple e− symbols mark an electron-transfer pathway, with a blue arrow showing electrons moving through the protein toward an external partner on the right.\n\nThe main message is that structural rearrangements gate access to the cofactor and regulate the timing and direction of coupled proton and electron flow. Rather than showing a static active site, the diagram emphasizes dynamic control: changes in protein conformation alter the local environment of the metal center and associated side chains, which in turn modulates redox chemistry and communication with surrounding molecules.",
